const ficinoSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('approach','A translator-philosopher, not a one-line founder',[
    'Marsilio Ficino matters because he changed what educated Latin readers could read and how they could imagine the relation between ancient philosophy and Christian life. He translated the complete Platonic corpus available to him, then Plotinus and many later Platonists, while composing arguments, summaries, commentaries, letters, sermons, medical advice, and theological works of his own. Translation was not preliminary clerical labor before the “real” philosophy. Choosing vocabulary, ordering texts, writing introductions, and resolving dangerous ambiguities were themselves philosophical acts. His Plato became a Renaissance Plato: ancient, but interpreted through late Platonism, scholastic theology, humanist rhetoric, pastoral concern, and the needs of patrons and students.',
    'Calling Ficino simply the founder of Renaissance Platonism hides both predecessors and plurality. Greek texts, Byzantine teachers, Italian humanists, medieval Platonizing traditions, and Medici patronage all preceded or enabled his achievement. Nor did one formally chartered Florentine Academy transmit a single doctrine under his command. Ficino used “academy” for Plato’s writings, pedagogical activity, and networks of conversation; surviving evidence supports patronage, houses, lessons, correspondence, and gatherings, but not the later picture of a continuous institution modeled on a modern university. His historical importance lies in an unusually productive intersection of texts, friendships, print, religious vocation, and philosophical invention—not an immaculate institutional founding.',
  ],['platonism','neoplatonism','philosophy-of-religion'],['plato','plotinus'],['Platonis Opera Omnia','Platonic Theology']),
  section('life-network','Florence, patronage, priesthood, and intellectual friendship',[
    'Born at Figline Valdarno in 1433, Ficino was the son of the physician Dietifeci Ficino. The details of his early education and Greek training remain partly uncertain, although his writings show substantial formation in Latin, medicine, scholastic philosophy, and humanist culture. Cosimo de’ Medici became his decisive patron in the 1460s, providing property and supporting the translation of Greek philosophical texts. That relationship gave Ficino time, manuscripts, status, and an audience, but patronage was neither permanent security nor intellectual ownership. After Cosimo’s death, Ficino negotiated changing relations with Lorenzo de’ Medici and other patrons, supported himself through ecclesiastical and teaching roles, and cultivated correspondents far beyond Florence.',
    'Ficino was ordained a priest in 1473 and later became a canon of Florence’s cathedral. He presented philosophy as care for minds and souls while also drawing on the medical world of his father. Letters advise friends about grief, character, study, health, and devotion; sermons carried learned themes into religious settings. His social circle included distinguished humanists such as Angelo Poliziano and Giovanni Pico della Mirandola, yet Ficino explicitly resisted claiming every associate as a pupil. He described some as patrons, some as conversational friends, and some as hearers or students. This distinction matters: intellectual exchange in Florence was collaborative, competitive, and status-laden. “Ficino’s academy” is best understood as a flexible program of Platonic study and ethical friendship supported by several institutions and households.',
  ],['neoplatonism','philosophy-of-religion'],[],['Letters']),
  section('translation','Recovering Plato and Plotinus through interpretive translation',[
    'Ficino completed drafts of Latin translations of Plato’s extant corpus by about 1468–69 and published the collection in 1484. The volume included thirty-five dialogues and letters arranged through the traditional nine tetralogies, including works modern scholars regard as spurious. Readers should not blame him for failing to apply later philological judgments; the received corpus and ancient authorities framed his task. His short argumenta and later commentaries directed readers toward theological and metaphysical coherence, often using Plotinus and other late Platonists to disclose Plato’s supposedly deepest intent. The resulting Latin Plato was extraordinarily consequential because translation widened access while interpretation shaped what that access meant.',
    'Ficino’s later translation and commentary on Plotinus, printed in 1492, performed a related but different intervention. Plotinus offered a powerful hierarchy of the One, Intellect, Soul, and the sensible world, yet Ficino adapted that philosophy to Christian creation, providence, angels, personal immortality, and salvation. He also translated Hermetic writings and works associated with Iamblichus, Proclus, Porphyry, Synesius, and Psellus. This activity should not be narrated as the simple recovery of a pure classical archive. Each corpus arrived through manuscripts, traditional attributions, editorial choices, and a Christianizing explanatory frame. Ficino transmitted ancient philosophy by transforming it, and that creative mediation is both the source of his importance and a reason to consult modern critical editions.',
  ],['platonism','neoplatonism'],['plato','plotinus','porphyry','iamblichus','proclus'],['Platonis Opera Omnia','Plotini Opera']),
  section('ancient-theology','Prisca theologia as a history of convergent revelation',[
    'Ficino’s prisca theologia, or ancient theology, pictured true wisdom unfolding through a succession of ancient sages. Versions of the sequence included Zoroaster, Hermes Trismegistus, Orpheus, Aglaophemus, Pythagoras, Philolaus, and Plato. The order changed across his career, and Ficino did not treat every pagan claim as equally sound. The scheme authorized a broad historical practice: poetry, ritual language, philosophy, and Christian scripture could be read as partial witnesses to one providential truth. It also let him argue that Platonism prepared readers for Christianity rather than threatening it. Philosophy remained subordinate to theology, but ancient interpreters could help repair a religion Ficino feared had become separated from learned wisdom.',
    'Modern readers must preserve the historical error built into this framework. Ficino and many contemporaries dated writings attributed to Hermes Trismegistus far earlier than current scholarship does. Isaac Casaubon’s seventeenth-century philology later undermined that chronology; the Hermetic texts are products of late antiquity, not primeval Egyptian records predating Moses. The mistake does not make Ficino’s project meaningless, because prisca theologia was also a method of comparison, authorization, and spiritual pedagogy. But it prevents treating his succession as factual intellectual genealogy. The “one ancient theology” was a Renaissance construction assembled from texts of different dates, religions, genres, and purposes. Its unity belongs to Ficino’s interpretive argument rather than to a recoverable ancient institution.',
  ],['philosophy-of-religion','platonism'],['pythagoras','plato'],['Pimander','On the Christian Religion']),
  section('soul','Soul as the bond of a living hierarchy',[
    'The Platonic Theology organizes much of reality through five levels: God, angelic mind, rational soul, quality, and body. Ficino varies the arrangement in places, so it should not be turned into a diagram more rigid than his writing. Its persistent center is soul. Soul cleaves upward to intelligible forms and divine life while governing nature and embodied activity below; it is the “bond” or “knot” connecting otherwise distant regions. This mediating role supports both dignity and difficulty. Human beings can understand, desire, create, and range imaginatively through the cosmos, but embodiment also scatters attention among unstable sensible things and can obscure the soul’s own character.',
    'Ficino’s long defense of immortality responds to materialist and Averroist positions as he understood them, and it uses many kinds of argument rather than one modern demonstration. The soul’s universal desire, intellectual capacity, freedom, relation to forms, and inability to be reduced to a bodily organ all contribute. Christian commitments and scriptural allusions stand beside Platonic, Aristotelian, and scholastic materials. Calling the work a synthesis is accurate if synthesis means a deliberately varied treasury, not a frictionless system. Ficino’s soul is individual and personally immortal, unlike interpretations of one shared intellect that seemed to endanger personal survival. Yet the proof remains contested, and his rhetoric often seeks moral conversion as well as logical assent.',
  ],['metaphysics','philosophy-of-mind','philosophy-of-religion'],['plotinus','aquinas'],['Platonic Theology']),
  section('love','Love, beauty, friendship, and ascent',[
    'Ficino’s Commentary on Plato’s Symposium, often called De amore, is staged as a renewed banquet in which friends deliver speeches on Plato’s dialogue. Its literary form matters. Different speakers allow a cumulative ascent through beauty, desire, soul, cosmic order, and divine goodness without making every formulation a detachable axiom. Love is a movement toward the good under the appearance of beauty. Sensible beauty can awaken the soul, but the task is to pass from fascination with one body toward beauty of soul, intelligible order, and ultimately God. “Platonic love” in Ficino is therefore neither merely sexless affection nor a modern romantic category.',
    'The ascent does not simply condemn bodies. Visible harmony can function as sign and invitation because a providential cosmos bears traces of intelligible beauty. Yet desire can also stop at possession, appearance, or self-projection. Ficino’s treatment of friendship and likeness explores how lovers may seek themselves in another and how shared orientation toward the good can redirect that impulse. Gendered assumptions, elite male sociability, and spiritual hierarchies limit the universality that later readers sometimes claim for the text. Its reception in art, literature, courtly culture, and theories of love greatly exceeded technical philosophy, often selecting attractive vocabulary while detaching it from Ficino’s Christian theology and demanding account of purification.',
  ],['ethics','aesthetics','philosophy-of-religion'],['plato'],['Commentary on Plato’s Symposium on Love']),
  section('astrology-magic','Astrology, medicine, spirit, and natural magic',[
    'Three Books on Life begins from the health of scholars. Sedentary study, melancholy, diet, sleep, music, odor, medicine, and the surrounding environment all enter a practical regimen. Ficino accepted humoral medicine and astrology as learned disciplines, not as marginal superstitions accidentally attached to an otherwise modern philosophy. Celestial bodies, natural materials, imagination, and a subtle spiritus were thought to participate in networks of affinity. The third book asks how a person might obtain life from the heavens through properly chosen foods, sounds, images, stones, and times. Such practices presuppose a living, ensouled cosmos very different from later mechanical nature.',
    'Ficino repeatedly distinguishes natural attraction from illicit demonic invocation, but the boundary is neither obvious nor historically uncontested. His discussion of talismanic images and celestial powers drew concern after publication in 1489, prompting explanations and appeals to allies at the papal court. It would be misleading to call him simply a magician, since medicine, commentary on Plotinus, prayer, music, and philosophical cosmology interweave throughout the work. It would be equally misleading to sanitize the text into metaphor. Ficino seriously entertained causal powers modern science rejects. The responsible interpretation asks how he tried to define legitimate natural magic within Christian limits, why critics doubted that limit, and how practice related to his theory of cosmic mediation.',
  ],['philosophy-of-science','philosophy-of-religion'],['plotinus'],['Three Books on Life']),
  section('tensions','Orthodoxy, pagan materials, and interpretive tensions',[
    'Ficino consistently presented his work as Christian, yet his archive created tensions he did not dissolve. Late antique polytheist metaphysics, Hermetic revelations, astrology, theurgy, biblical exegesis, sacramental analogy, and scholastic theology could reinforce one another under his providential history, but they could also conflict. He sometimes used qualification, allegory, hierarchy, or genre to contain dangerous claims. His priesthood should not be treated as proof that every experiment was institutionally approved, while the 1490 concern over Three Books on Life should not be inflated into a dramatic persecution narrative. The evidence indicates scrutiny and successful defense, not a Galileo-like trial or a clean victory of occultism over church authority.',
    'Other tensions are philosophical. The material world is good as divine creation and yet a source of instability and forgetfulness. Soul dignifies humanity but can encourage anthropocentric confidence. Love educates desire yet emerges from socially exclusive settings. Prisca theologia welcomes multiple ancient witnesses while subordinating them to a Christian fulfillment. Ficino’s historical influence is broad, but direct causal lines to every later Platonist, artist, magician, or scientist cannot be presumed. He supplied texts and concepts that later readers selected, criticized, or rearranged. A careful legacy distinguishes availability, explicit citation, shared vocabulary, and retrospective resemblance rather than calling all four “influence.”',
  ],['philosophy-of-religion','metaphysics'],[],['Three Books on Life','On the Christian Religion']),
  section('legacy-reading','How to read Ficino now',[
    'Ficino’s most durable achievement may be the creation of a Latin Platonic library joined to an interpretive program. His editions helped Plato, Plotinus, and later Platonists circulate through early modern Europe; his vocabulary of love, soul, cosmic sympathy, and ancient theology traveled through literary, religious, medical, and philosophical settings. Later historians sometimes dismissed him for corrupting Plato with Neoplatonic and Christian additions. That criticism identifies real mediation but mistakes the nature of Renaissance commentary if it expects modern historicism. Ficino did not aim to reconstruct a sealed ancient intention. He aimed to make ancient wisdom speak truthfully and therapeutically to his present. Understanding that goal permits critique without condescension.',
    'A beginner can start with selected letters or the Symposium commentary because their situations make Ficino’s philosophy of friendship and ascent tangible. The Platonic Theology then reveals the scale of his metaphysics and immortality project, especially books 3, 9, 14, and 18, but it rewards guidance through its many authorities and rhetorical registers. Three Books on Life should come next as a corrective to a purely spiritual portrait: it places bodies, work habits, music, medicine, stars, and contested magic inside philosophy. Throughout, compare Ficino’s claims with the ancient text he interprets and with modern scholarship. The goal is not to choose between “humanist translator” and “occult philosopher,” but to see why those activities belonged together for him.',
  ],['platonism','neoplatonism','philosophy-of-religion'],['plato','plotinus'],['Letters','Commentary on Plato’s Symposium on Love','Platonic Theology','Three Books on Life']),
  section('why-matters','Why Ficino belongs in an atlas of development',[
    'Ficino makes intellectual development visible because ancient works do not travel by themselves. Manuscripts must be acquired, languages learned, vocabularies chosen, patrons persuaded, printers engaged, readers trained, and apparent contradictions interpreted. His career lets visitors follow a chain from Plato and late ancient Platonists through Byzantine and Italian transmission into Renaissance Latin reading. At every link, preservation also produces change. The Latin archive he assembled enabled new arguments while carrying Ficino’s chronology, Christianity, explanatory summaries, and preferred late-Platonic harmonies into the encounter. That is a more historically useful legacy than the isolated title “Renaissance Platonist.”',
    'His work also refuses the later separation of philosophy into sealed specialties. A theory of soul becomes an account of cosmic causation, personal immortality, love, education, health, music, prayer, and celestial influence. This breadth can inspire connections, but it also raises standards of evidence: philosophical coherence does not make an astrological therapy causally sound, and theological purpose does not authenticate a historical genealogy. Ficino is therefore a productive guide to both synthesis and criticism. Readers learn how a worldview can join many practices meaningfully while still depending on claims that later textual scholarship or natural investigation overturns.',
  ],['platonism','neoplatonism','philosophy-of-religion'],['plato','plotinus'],['Platonic Theology','Three Books on Life']),
];

