const machiavelliSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('problem','The thinker behind the adjective',[
    'Machiavelli’s name became an adjective for calculating deceit long before many readers encountered his books. That reputation is not invented from nothing: The Prince openly examines cruelty, deception, fear, force, and the ability to act against conventional goodness when political survival demands it. Yet “the ends justify the means” does not appear as his maxim, and the slogan erases the questions that organize his corpus: which ends, whose security, what kind of state, under what institutions, and with what long-term consequences? Effective violence can destroy authority; a feared ruler must avoid hatred; acquisitions that cannot be maintained expose incapacity rather than virtù.',
    'The Discourses on Livy makes the reduction still less defensible. There Machiavelli studies republican liberty, popular judgment, social conflict, citizen arms, law, corruption, founding, religion, expansion, and renewal. The Prince and Discourses are neither easily identical nor safely assigned to opposite “true” and “false” Machiavellis. Both ask how political orders begin and endure under necessity and contingency; they often shift perspective between founders, princes, elites, peoples, and institutions. Their genre, audience, chronology, and rhetoric differ, leaving genuine interpretive disputes. Reading Machiavelli well means holding practical candor, ethical danger, republican commitment, and literary strategy together rather than rescuing or condemning him through one sentence.',
  ],['political-philosophy'],['livy'],['The Prince','Discourses on Livy']),
  section('life-office','Republican office, diplomacy, defeat, and writing',[
    'Born in Florence in 1469, Machiavelli entered public service in 1498 after the fall of Savonarola. As second chancellor and secretary to the Ten of War, he drafted official correspondence, traveled on diplomatic missions, studied rulers and military systems, and worked under the republican leadership of Piero Soderini. His reports on France, the papacy, the emperor, and Cesare Borgia supplied cases later transformed in his political writing. Experience did not simply dictate conclusions: missions produced partial observations under pressure, while ancient histories and humanist rhetorical education supplied comparative frameworks. Machiavelli’s political thought emerged from the traffic between office, reading, persuasion, and retrospective diagnosis.',
    'The republic collapsed when Medici power returned with Spanish and papal support in 1512. Machiavelli lost office; in 1513 he was arrested, imprisoned, and tortured after being implicated in a conspiracy, then released under an amnesty. At his farm near Sant’Andrea in Percussina he wrote The Prince and began the work that became the Discourses, seeking both intellectual explanation and a path back into public usefulness. He later wrote plays, poetry, military dialogue, diplomatic and historical works, including the Florentine Histories commissioned by Medici authorities. Partial rehabilitation complicates any picture of permanent republican martyrdom. He died in 1527, shortly after another expulsion of the Medici, without returning to his earlier republican office.',
  ],['political-philosophy'],[],['Legations','The Prince','Discourses on Livy','Florentine Histories']),
  section('prince','The Prince: acquisition, preservation, and exposed judgment',[
    'The Prince classifies principalities and studies the distinct problems of hereditary, mixed, new, civil, and ecclesiastical rule. Its sharpest analysis concerns new states, where inherited loyalty is weak and the founder must create expectations, defenses, and institutions. Cesare Borgia supplies admired examples of resolution and criticized failures of final judgment; no character is a simple model. Machiavelli’s “effectual truth” turns from imagined republics and principalities toward what people actually do, but it does not eliminate evaluation. He asks whether actions secure a state, maintain support, control armed force, and avoid dependencies that later make rule collapse. Apparent success can conceal structural weakness.',
    'The notorious counsel to learn how not to be good is conditional and political. Generosity can require taxation that breeds hatred; mercy can permit disorders that harm many; honesty can fail among faithless competitors. A ruler must know both law and force and appear faithful, humane, religious, and upright even when necessity requires contrary action. This account is ethically disturbing because public appearance becomes an instrument and necessity is open to self-serving interpretation. Yet Machiavelli also imposes limits: cruelty should be concentrated rather than continually renewed, property and women should not be seized, promises cannot be broken without reasons and management of reputation, and hatred endangers rule. He analyzes wrongdoing; whether, when, and how far he endorses it remains contested.',
  ],['political-philosophy'],[],['The Prince']),
  section('virtu-fortuna','Virtù, fortuna, necessity, and timing',[
    'Virtù is not equivalent to moral virtue, villainy, masculinity, or generic skill. It names a capacity to read circumstances, command resources, shape institutions, persuade, fight, and alter conduct as times change. The quality can appear in founders, military leaders, citizens, and peoples, though The Prince often uses gendered and violent language around it. Fortuna names the field of contingency that escapes complete control: inherited conditions, accidents, shifting alliances, opportunity, and the unpredictable responses of others. Machiavelli’s river image captures their relation. Floods cannot always be prevented, but preparation before the crisis can channel damage. Virtù works on fortune without abolishing it.',
    'Success therefore cannot establish moral desert or permanent mastery. A temperament suited to one moment may fail when circumstances reverse; people rarely know how to change their habitual mode in time. Impetuosity and caution are not universal recipes. Machiavelli’s examples also expose the role of opportunity: Moses, Cyrus, Romulus, and Theseus required disordered conditions in which founding became possible, while opportunity without capacity would have been wasted. Necessità likewise ranges from literal compulsion to pressures a political actor describes as unavoidable. Readers must ask whether necessity is objective, institutionally produced, rhetorically invoked, or retrospectively claimed. That ambiguity is central to the danger and analytical power of Machiavelli’s political vocabulary.',
  ],['political-philosophy'],[],['The Prince','Discourses on Livy']),
  section('republic','Republican liberty and the political value of conflict',[
    'The Discourses uses Livy’s Rome as an argumentative laboratory for republics. Machiavelli distinguishes living securely under a well-ordered prince from living freely through participation in a self-governing community. Liberty is not merely private noninterference. It depends on laws, offices, public speech, military capacity, and arrangements that prevent one person or social group from commanding at will. Machiavelli often entrusts guardianship of liberty to the people because ordinary citizens chiefly desire not to be dominated, whereas elites desire to dominate. That contrast is stylized, not a claim that every popular decision is wise or every noble identical.',
    'Rome’s tumults between plebs and senate produced laws favorable to liberty. This claim overturns the assumption that social harmony is always the mark of healthy politics. Conflict can reveal competing desires and, when channeled through assemblies, tribunes, accusations, and public debate, constrain elite capture. Machiavelli does not celebrate every riot, faction, or civil war. Conflict becomes destructive when private loyalties replace public contest, accusations give way to slander, offices become spoils, or groups seek domination rather than lawful redress. His republicanism is therefore institutional and conflictual: liberty requires neither unanimity nor uncontrolled violence, but durable forms through which unequal social forces can oppose one another publicly.',
  ],['political-philosophy'],['livy'],['Discourses on Livy']),
  section('corruption-renewal','Corruption, founding, law, and periodic renewal',[
    'Corruption in Machiavelli is broader than bribery. A corrupted city has lost the habits, expectations, social equality, and attachment to public institutions that let laws operate. Citizens pursue private advantage through factions; powerful families capture offices; ordinary people cease to trust public remedies; and legal forms survive after their supporting ways of life have decayed. Laws designed for a healthy republic may fail among corrupted people, while extraordinary remedies can themselves authorize tyranny. This diagnosis explains why institutional copying is insufficient. Rome’s arrangements cannot simply be installed in Florence without considering material conditions, military organization, class relations, memory, and the distribution of power.',
    'Political orders nevertheless require beginnings and renewals. Founders may need concentrated authority to establish laws, but a founder who keeps power for private heirs differs from one who orders a republic capable of outliving him. Later renewal can occur through legal institutions, exemplary actions, external shocks, or a return to animating principles. Machiavelli’s admiration for founding severity creates one of his hardest tensions: extraordinary action may protect public liberty, yet people who claim exceptional necessity are a recurring threat to it. The Discourses does not provide a safe algorithm for dictatorship. It teaches readers to examine duration, succession, accountability, and whether exceptional power returns authority to public orders or converts emergency into domination.',
  ],['political-philosophy'],['livy'],['Discourses on Livy']),
  section('arms-empire','Citizen arms, mercenaries, expansion, and imperial costs',[
    'Machiavelli’s insistence on “one’s own arms” unites The Prince, Discourses, and Art of War. Mercenaries and auxiliary troops answer to their pay, commanders, or foreign patrons rather than to the polity whose survival is at stake. His own role in organizing a Florentine militia gave this issue practical urgency, although the militia’s defeat in 1512 complicates triumphal claims. Citizen soldiers can connect defense to liberty and reduce dependence on military entrepreneurs, but armed citizenship also raises questions of discipline, social inclusion, elite command, and the treatment of subject populations. Machiavelli’s military argument is institutional, not romantic confidence that courage alone wins wars.',
    'Rome’s liberty is bound to expansion in the Discourses. Popular participation and citizen arms generate energies that help a republic acquire territory, while expansion feeds conflict, wealth, inequality, and the risk of military command becoming personal power. Machiavelli often prefers expansive Rome to stable Sparta or Venice, but admiration does not erase the costs borne by conquered peoples. His framework largely evaluates empire from the durability and greatness of the conquering republic. Modern readers should neither hide that perspective nor assume it settles contemporary questions. The analysis remains valuable because it exposes a structural problem: the capacities that protect a free polity can be redirected outward as domination and return inward through commanders, wealth, and corrupted dependence.',
  ],['political-philosophy'],[],['The Art of War','Discourses on Livy']),
  section('religion-rhetoric','Religion, rhetoric, gender, and moral controversy',[
    'Machiavelli treats religion as political practice, historical force, and field of judgment. Roman religion helped bind oaths, discipline armies, authorize institutions, and sustain civic confidence. He criticizes forms of Christianity that, in his reading, glorify humility and passive endurance at the expense of worldly vigor, while elsewhere attacking corrupt clergy and recognizing religion’s capacity to organize common life. Scholars dispute his personal belief and the sincerity of his pious language. The safest conclusion is not that religion is merely fraud. His political analysis asks how beliefs, ceremonies, authorities, and interpretations shape action, and how rulers can manipulate them without controlling every consequence.',
    'Rhetoric is equally central. The Prince’s dramatic examples, provocations, maxims, and shifts of address are designed interventions, not neutral data tables. The Discourses praises public speech and stages arguments through Roman history. Gendered images of feminized weakness and fortuna as a woman to be beaten are not incidental ornaments; they help define an aggressive political capacity and carry violence into the conceptual scheme. Readers may distinguish historical language from present endorsement without pretending the imagery has no philosophical effect. Moral controversy therefore cannot be closed by calling Machiavelli a scientist, satirist, republican, or teacher of evil. Each label illuminates evidence and suppresses other evidence unless tested across genres.',
  ],['political-philosophy','philosophy-of-religion'],[],['The Prince','Discourses on Livy','Mandragola']),
  section('legacy-reading','Reception and a responsible reading path',[
    'Machiavelli’s reception produced anti-Machiavel polemic, reason-of-state debate, republican appropriation, realist theory, democratic readings, and repeated arguments about modernity. Later thinkers encountered both texts and a cultural character called “Machiavelli,” sometimes attacking a doctrine he never stated. Direct influence must be established work by work; resemblance to political realism is not proof. His break with moralized mirrors-for-princes literature is significant, but ancient historians, Roman political vocabulary, Florentine humanism, medieval institutions, and Christian controversy remain inside his writing. Calling him the first modern political scientist turns a contested transformation into a solitary invention and overlooks how historical example, rhetoric, and normative preference structure his analysis.',
    'Begin with The Prince, but read chapters 6–9, 12–14, 15–19, 21, and 25–26 rather than extracting chapters on cruelty and fear alone. Then turn immediately to Discourses I.2–6, I.16–18, I.29–34, II.2, and III.1 to encounter regime cycles, tumults, liberty, corruption, arms, and renewal. The Art of War and Florentine Histories widen the institutional and historical picture; letters and plays restore the writer’s changing voices. Track virtù, fortuna, stato, ordine, popolo, grandi, vivere sicuro, and vivere libero across translations. The best reading leaves a productive discomfort: Machiavelli reveals failures of conventional goodness without guaranteeing that effective power deserves obedience or admiration.',
  ],['political-philosophy'],['hobbes','rousseau'],['The Prince','Discourses on Livy','The Art of War','Florentine Histories']),
  section('why-matters','Political judgment without a comforting guarantee',[
    'Machiavelli remains unsettling because he denies that worthy character, lawful inheritance, superior numbers, or pious intention guarantees political success. Outcomes depend on timing, organization, perception, armed capacity, institutions, and responses that no actor fully controls. This recognition can deepen responsibility: leaders must judge consequences and prepare for foreseeable danger. It can also become an alibi, because necessity is easy to exaggerate and effective action can be confused with whatever the powerful survive doing. His examples train suspicion toward both moral self-congratulation and victorious self-justification.',
    'The republican writings supply a public standard that limits fascination with exceptional leaders. Ask whether citizens retain institutions for accusation, speech, office, defense, and resistance to domination; whether conflict creates law or private faction; and whether emergency power returns authority to the community. These questions do not make Machiavelli a contemporary democrat, and his acceptance of conquest, hierarchy, gendered domination, and harsh founding acts requires criticism. They explain why his corpus remains more than cynical advice. It is an inquiry into political capacities whose value changes with the ends and orders they serve.',
  ],['political-philosophy'],['hobbes','rousseau'],['The Prince','Discourses on Livy']),
];

