const baconSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('myth-method','A reformer of inquiry, not inventor of one scientific method',[
    'Francis Bacon is often credited with inventing “the scientific method,” as if modern science followed a single recipe first written in the Novum Organum. That story mistakes a large, unfinished reform for a classroom flowchart. Bacon criticized premature generalization, inherited authority, scholastic disputation, private intellectual heroics, and collections of observations made without a plan. In their place he proposed disciplined natural and experimental histories, ordered comparisons, exclusion of alternatives, gradual movement toward axioms, and repeated testing through new works. His induction was not merely “observe many cases, then generalize,” and his project included institutions, instruments, labor, language, funding, classification, and public use.',
    'Bacon also did not create experiment, observation, induction, useful arts, or collaborative knowledge from nothing. Artisans, physicians, alchemists, natural historians, navigators, mathematicians, and earlier philosophers supplied practices and materials he selectively reorganized. He sometimes criticized their secrecy, haste, accidental discovery, or narrow aims while borrowing from their achievements. Nor do later sciences simply enact his detailed procedure. His importance lies in making the reform of knowledge an ambitious philosophical and political program: inquiry should be deliberately organized to correct the mind, accumulate reliable materials, disclose natural powers, and produce works that relieve human life. That program inspired later institutions even where Bacon’s own natural philosophy was abandoned.',
  ],['philosophy-of-science','empiricism'],[],['Novum Organum','Instauratio magna']),
  section('life-politics','Law, court politics, office, disgrace, and writing',[
    'Born in London in 1561 into a politically connected family, Bacon studied at Trinity College, Cambridge, and Gray’s Inn, then pursued intertwined legal, parliamentary, court, and literary careers. His opposition to aspects of received Aristotelian and scholastic learning did not mean he rejected every ancient argument or university practice. Humanist rhetoric, history, law, diplomacy, scripture, natural magic, medicine, and mechanical arts all informed his reform. Under Elizabeth I his advancement was uneven and his association with the Earl of Essex ended painfully when Bacon participated in the prosecution after Essex’s rebellion. Under James I he rose through solicitor general, attorney general, lord keeper, and lord chancellor.',
    'In 1621 Parliament accused Bacon of accepting gifts from litigants. He confessed, was fined, imprisoned briefly, and barred from office, though the fine and imprisonment were remitted. Historians debate the precise conventions, motives, and consequences surrounding the charges; his defense that gifts did not change judgments does not erase the conflict of interest. Political disgrace gave him more time for writing but did not suddenly create the philosophical project, which had occupied him for decades. He died in 1626. The later story that he contracted his fatal illness while stuffing a chicken with snow illustrates the hazards of memorable biography: it derives from an early report and may preserve an experiment, but should not carry more evidential weight than the documented career and corpus.',
  ],['philosophy-of-science','political-philosophy'],[],['Essays','Novum Organum']),
  section('instauration','The Great Instauration as an unfinished architecture',[
    'The Instauratio magna was projected in six parts: a survey and division of learning; the new organon or interpretive method; natural and experimental histories; worked examples of the method; preliminary findings; and a completed operative philosophy. Bacon never finished this architecture. The Novum Organum supplies two books of aphorisms rather than the promised complete logic. Natural histories and methodological fragments occupy much of the surviving effort, while the final philosophy remains aspirational. Incompletion is philosophically important. It prevents readers from treating preliminary rules, isolated examples, or later editorial groupings as a finished system that Bacon himself successfully demonstrated across nature.',
    'The scale also explains Bacon’s rejection of solitary genius. No individual could collect, verify, order, and interpret the required materials. A durable division of intellectual labor would assign observation, experiment, compilation, comparison, theoretical ascent, and practical testing without severing them. Bacon imagined aids that discipline judgment much as tools extend the hand. Method was therefore both cognitive and administrative. Yet administration introduces its own risks: who decides which facts enter a history, how negative results are preserved, which inventions count as benefits, and who may direct collective power? Bacon recognized some distortions of authority and secrecy, but his confidence in organized inquiry leaves modern readers responsible for questions of accountability his program does not fully answer.',
  ],['philosophy-of-science','epistemology'],[],['Instauratio magna','Novum Organum']),
  section('idols','The idols: recurring distortions of inquiry',[
    'The four idols name sources of error that cannot be cured by collecting more facts alone. Idols of the tribe arise from shared human tendencies: imagining more order than evidence warrants, favoring confirmations, and projecting the senses or mind onto nature. Idols of the cave reflect individual education, temperament, habit, and favored authors. Idols of the marketplace arise through words formed for ordinary exchange that mislead technical inquiry. Idols of the theater are staged philosophical systems and demonstrations whose internal elegance can conceal poor foundations. The taxonomy is not a modern catalog of experimentally verified cognitive biases, though it can prompt comparisons. It belongs to Bacon’s polemic and theory of interpretation.',
    'Bacon does not promise a mind purified into passive neutrality. Inquiry requires actively designed aids: definitions tied to operations, diverse instances, deliberate variation, written histories, tables, exclusions, and communities able to challenge premature commitments. The marketplace idol is especially important because language does not merely label an independently settled world; inherited words can divide phenomena badly or suggest nonexistent things. Theater idols likewise warn that error can be institutional and systematic rather than an individual lapse. Bacon’s own framework must be exposed to the same test. His matter theory, religious assumptions, social hierarchy, and confidence in technological benefit are not exempt simply because he named common sources of deception.',
  ],['epistemology','philosophy-of-language','philosophy-of-science'],[],['Novum Organum']),
  section('induction','Induction, tables, exclusions, and ascending axioms',[
    'Baconian induction proceeds through managed comparison rather than simple enumeration. For a nature such as heat, the investigator collects instances where it is present, instances apparently similar where it is absent, and cases where it varies by degree. The tables do not mechanically deliver a law. They support exclusion: proposed natures incompatible with the pattern are rejected until a provisional “first vintage” remains. Additional privileged instances, new experiments, and deductive consequences then test and refine the result. Bacon calls the sought explanatory structures “forms,” but these are not straightforward copies of Platonic Forms or modern equations. Their meaning is tied to repeatable processes and powers for producing effects.',
    'The ascent should move gradually from particulars through intermediate axioms to wider generalities, then descend to new works and experiments. This reciprocal motion distinguishes interpretation of nature from both empiricism without structure and deduction from untested first principles. Bacon knew the method was difficult to execute, and his famous example of heat does not amount to a completed modern theory. Later science developed mathematical, probabilistic, hypothetical, model-based, and instrument-intensive practices that cannot be reduced to his tables. The fair claim is historical and philosophical: Bacon gave influential form to a program in which discovery requires systematic variation, negative evidence, controlled ascent, and the ability of explanations to generate further inquiry.',
  ],['philosophy-of-science','epistemology','empiricism'],[],['Novum Organum']),
  section('history-experiment','Natural history, experiment, and learned experience',[
    'For Bacon, natural history is the foundation of philosophy, not a cabinet of curiosities below theory. It should record nature in ordinary course, nature deviating in marvels or errors, and nature constrained and transformed by human art. Histories of winds, life and death, density and rarity, metals, plants, trades, and countless other topics would gather observations, recipes, failures, measurements, testimony, and designed trials. Bacon’s category of experiment includes interventions undertaken to learn, but also the disciplined reworking of artisanal and practical knowledge. Mere quantity is insufficient: facts must be selected and described for later comparison, with conditions and negative outcomes preserved.',
    'He distinguishes experiments of light, which disclose causes, from experiments of fruit, which immediately yield useful effects, while insisting that the two should ultimately cooperate. An apparently useless result can reorganize understanding and later enable works; a profitable trick can remain theoretically barren. This distinction resists the idea that Bacon valued technology but not truth, or truth but not application. Still, the planned histories could reproduce unreliable testimony, elite assumptions, and hidden craft appropriation. Bacon’s call to extract knowledge from workshops widened philosophy’s archive, but it did not automatically credit laborers as equal knowers. The social organization of evidence is thus both a strength and an unresolved ethical problem.',
  ],['philosophy-of-science'],[],['Novum Organum','Natural and Experimental Histories']),
  section('matter-cause','Forms, matter, motion, and the limits of the empiricist label',[
    'Bacon’s substantive natural philosophy does not resemble a modern world made only of measurable inert particles. He speculated about tangible and pneumatic matter, spirits within bodies, appetites, motions, textures, heat, cold, rarefaction, and transformations. His accounts changed across works and remain difficult to reconstruct. “Form” identifies a lawlike or structural condition through which a nature can be understood and produced, while latent processes and configurations explain transformations inaccessible to unaided sense. This ontology shows why calling Bacon simply an empiricist can mislead. Observation mattered, but so did causal hypotheses, metaphysical commitments, and interventions designed around what senses do not directly reveal.',
    'Bacon famously criticized excessive concentration on Aristotle’s four causes, especially final causes in physics, but he did not ban purpose from every field. He relocated questions according to the divisions of learning and sought material-efficient explanations that could support operation. Mathematics had a role but not the sovereign place it would have in Galilean and Newtonian physics; Bacon sometimes feared that elegant abstraction would outrun physical investigation. His program therefore represents one route through early modern natural philosophy, not the blueprint later science uniformly selected. It can illuminate why methods and ontologies develop together: a table of instances makes sense only within assumptions about which natures, processes, and differences are causally significant.',
  ],['philosophy-of-science','metaphysics','empiricism'],['aristotle'],['Novum Organum','History of Dense and Rare']),
  section('institution-power','Salomon’s House, collaboration, secrecy, and technological power',[
    'The unfinished New Atlantis imagines Bensalem and its research institution, Salomon’s House. Specialized investigators travel, collect books and inventions, conduct experiments, compile results, and decide what to reveal to the state and public. The celebrated catalog of caves, towers, gardens, engines, medicines, optical devices, sounds, and artificial materials gives spatial form to Bacon’s conviction that knowledge should extend human power. Later readers saw an anticipation of research societies and laboratories. The resemblance is real but should not become a founding myth: the Royal Society and other institutions had multiple sources, and Bensalem is a literary, religious, and political fiction rather than a constitutional plan.',
    'Its secrecy is as important as its collaboration. Salomon’s House controls travel, information, expertise, and disclosure; the public receives benefits without transparent participation in setting research ends. Bacon’s formula about knowledge and power is therefore not automatically emancipatory. New capacities can relieve suffering, expand food and medicine, or protect life, but they can also intensify hierarchy, surveillance, extraction, warfare, and environmental domination. Bacon usually frames operative knowledge as charitable restoration of human dominion after the Fall, constrained by religion and public benefit. Modern reception must preserve that theological aim while examining whose “benefit” counts and how institutional power can be governed rather than merely increased.',
  ],['philosophy-of-science','political-philosophy'],[],['New Atlantis']),
  section('legacy-reading','Political tensions, legacy, and a reading path',[
    'Bacon did not apply one natural-scientific method unchanged to morality and politics. Civil government rests on authority, consent, reputation, custom, and opinion; abrupt improvement can destabilize it. His essays condense practical judgments about counsel, faction, friendship, empire, studies, truth, and office, often through crafted aphorism rather than demonstration. His career gives these themes uncomfortable force. A champion of intellectual reform participated in patronage, prosecution, colonial and imperial projects, and a gift-taking political culture. Neither philosophical achievement nor disgrace cancels the other. The same attention to institutional incentives that enriches his science should shape judgment of his politics and reception.',
    'Begin with Novum Organum I.1–130, where idols, hope, gradual ascent, and criticism of premature systems appear together. Continue into book II far enough to see tables, exclusions, provisional interpretation, and privileged instances; otherwise “Baconian induction” remains a slogan. Read selected natural histories to encounter the unruly materials, then The Advancement of Learning for disciplinary architecture and New Atlantis for the institutional imagination. Gaukroger and current specialist reference work help separate Bacon’s actual corpus from later “Baconian” identities. His most useful legacy is not a fixed recipe but a demanding question: what cognitive, material, linguistic, and political arrangements let communities discover dependable powers without mistaking organization for innocence?',
  ],['philosophy-of-science','political-philosophy','empiricism'],[],['Novum Organum','The Advancement of Learning','New Atlantis','Essays']),
  section('why-matters','Inquiry as designed collective responsibility',[
    'Bacon’s continuing challenge is that reliable inquiry requires more than brilliant ideas. It needs records that preserve failures, instruments that make differences observable, terms that can be corrected, incentives for sharing, divisions of labor that do not fragment responsibility, and institutions able to survive individual careers. Those requirements are familiar in contemporary research, yet their presence does not prove an institution trustworthy. Organization can magnify selective reporting, hierarchy, secrecy, and destructive aims as easily as it can correct individual bias. The idols migrate into systems built to defeat them.',
    'This institutional perspective also changes how technological power should be judged. Producing an effect can test understanding, but usefulness to a sponsor is not identical with public benefit, and control over nature does not settle questions of distribution or ecological cost. Bacon’s program offers intellectual resources for ambitious cooperation and for criticism of unsupported theory. Reading it beside his politics and New Atlantis makes the ethical task unavoidable: communities must design not only methods of discovery, but procedures for choosing ends, recognizing contributors, disclosing risk, and refusing powers whose promised relief conceals domination.',
  ],['philosophy-of-science','political-philosophy','empiricism'],[],['Novum Organum','New Atlantis']),
];

